This was my second ascension (first was a neutral barbarian).  Notable
aspects are the never changed form and artiwishless conducts, and the
fact that I *never* had a bag of holding.  I was feeling constrained
enough by not having a bag (I didn't even have a sack for much of the
game; more on that later) that I decided not to worry about other
conducts.  I am proud of the fact that I resisted wishing for a bag of
holding, though.

Valeria started out fairly normally.  I normally attempt to get to
minetown at a low level to try for protection, but generally have to
give up the protection run when my pet dies/falls through a hole--but
not this time!  An early wand of polymorph turned my pet into a master
mind flayer; I'm ashamed to say that my *very* first thought was,
"cool, he can kill the watch captain for me, so I'll have the silver
saber!"  Not exactly a lawful attitude, but, it turns out, exactly what
happened.

Minestown was a double bones pile, which was a mixed blessing.  On the
down side, most everyone was dead (other than Izchak and a general
store); on the up side, both of the previous players had died to
low-level monsters (a wood-elf killed an early archeologist, and it
looked like a wizard died playing with a fountain; there were water
moccasins around, and a heap of rings & wands on a fountain at the far
end).  Did what identifying I could, and since the priest was already
dead, I converted the altar (had been chaotic), dug out a fountain to
blank potions, and made some holy water, which allowed me to bless the
id scrolls I'd collected, which in turn helped me sort out the
rings--one of which was slow digestion, which I wore for most of the
rest of the game.  I also blessed the former arc's touchstone, but that
ended up being pointless--I never sold a gem, and I left them all
behind on the run.  Would've been different had I gotten a BoH, but
there it is.

After doing all I wanted to at the town, I went over to the sokoban
tower, where I picked up the amulet of reflection; once it was around
my neck, it never left (despite getting Vlad's amulet and another in
Rodney's tower).

I then headed back to the mines to get the luckstone, picking up two
more pets along the way; the wand turned them into a sasquatch and a
giant ant.  Oh, joy.  They actually lasted far longer than I expected,
though they did eventually die; unfortunately, the first pet to go was
my mmf, who got engulfed and insta-killed at mine's end.  *sigh*

I then turned around and started plowing through the dungeon.  I
converted every altar I found, and set up a camp at one on dlvl 8,
eventually getting Mjollnir, which made the plowing easier--too easy,
it turned out, as I got cocky and tried to take on a mind flayer in
melee.  Oops.  Slight pause to re-map levels and re-identify equipment,
and then charge on!

Medusa was the titan level, but he was peaceful, so no problems there;
of course, I didn't have any sort of levitation yet, so I dug down,
found the stairs, slapped on my blindfold, and stoned Medusa.  Perseus
gave me a pair of boots and a nice, shiny shield; I'd later find
another shield in a barracks, making three sources of reflection and no
BoH.  I could hear the RNG chuckling.  While floating around Medusa's
place, I ran into a battle with a troll & a gelatinous cube; I must've
really not been thinking because here's my train of thought: "My
hitpoints are dropping!  I'm burdened!  I need to drop my sack so I can
run away!  (Never mind that I could float a step or two back and be
surrounded by water...)  Oh, crap--I had a bunch of potions in that
bag, wonder if any of them survived?  Oh, well, I'll come back for it
once I'm healed.  Oh, crap!  The cube *ate* my sack!  Argh!"  So much
for the sack the archeologist had kindly left me in minetown; I could
hear the RNG actually laughing out loud.  Nothing for it but to keep
going, though.

Next stop was the castle, but when I got there, I realized I'd
forgotten to bring along any of the bugles I'd picked up in the
barracks, so I had to go in the back way.  None of the dragons dropped
scales, but a black one made me feel very firm, which was nice.  The
wand turned out to be 0:1 (seems to be a pattern for me), so it went to
3 blessed scrolls of charging, blessed +3 GDSM (got +0, and then
stopped to kill a blue dragon--who dropped scales.  The RNG chortled),
and 3 blessed scrolls of genocide (bye-bye h, L, and ;!).  Held on to
the last wishes, which turned out to be a good thing.

I headed back up to my camp (yep, the one all the way up on dlvl
8--without a BoH, I didn't feel like moving it) to enchant my armor.
Pulled out the magic marker I'd picked up from the former wizard in
minetown and a couple of blessed scrolls to write some ?oEA--only to
find that I'd forgotten what they looked like!  The RNG was positively
howling, I swear!  Out came the wand, and I soon had three blessed
scrolls of EA in hand, which allowed me to get my AC out of the "barely
negative" realm down to "decently comfortable".  I also performed some
alchemy to raise my abilities (I love blessed !ofGA!).

Next stop: help the Norn out.  Fell through a trapdoor on an upper
filler level and ended up below the locate level, which I posted about
here; that's now bug C343-200.  I must say, dealing with the valk's
locate level from the bottom up is an adventure--doing it normally, you
can use the cave mouth as a natural chokepoint, but coming up from
above, you can't help but get surrounded.  Managed to clear the hordes
eventually, popped back to the Norn to stash the loot (including
Ogresmasher, which a giant in the cave had), and then headed back down
to challenge Lord Surtur.  Who died in two hits.  Owie.  Nice that he's
not lightning resistant, but should he really be *that* easy?

At this point, I decided that I didn't like Mjollnir's tendency to
shatter rings, wands, and potions, so I camped out at an altar (I'd
learned create monster, which helped a bit) until Tyr saw fit to give
me Snickersnee.  Ah, well, it's better than nothing, and it doesn't
kill potions.  Time for Gehennom!

Ok, maybe not quite--I stopped in the valley to give lots of money to
the priest, since he was the first one I'd found all game.  Then I
popped on a ring of teleport control and started mapping Gehennom from
the bottom up, #invoking the Orb whenever I needed to get back to my
castle stash.  Found the VS on level 50 and worked my way up to 45,
where a giant spider dropped a sack--yes, the first randomly generated
bag (other than a bag of tricks) I'd found!  I also found a magic lamp
on the same level, which turned out to be pre-blessed.  Nifty.

All in all, I spent a lot of time in this part of the game, most of
which was probably not worthwhile, including another sac-fest which
resulted in Sunsword; I eventually decided to turn my starting sword
into Excalibur, and enchanted it up to +7--I must say, that
auto-searching is *very* nice.  I also spent some time polypiling,
which netted me a +oId and a three more genocide scrolls (bye-bye c and
R; I saved the third for a while).  With my robe, +5 HoB, and gauntlets
of dexterity, I could cast ID at 33% fail at level 15, which I found
rather useful.  I eventually made it to Orcus town, where I got my only
/oD; I also picked up a cloak of displacement and an oilskin sack.
After giving it some thought, I swapped out the +4 robe I'd been using
for the +5 cloak of displacement--I'd genocided all of the nasties that
make me prefer MC3 over MC2, and it's nice having monsters miss
frequently.  I kept the robe handy until the ascension run just so I
could cast ID when necessary.

The other demons went down very quickly (gotta love #twoweaponing with
a +7 silver saber!), and I soon found myself clearing out the wizard's
tower (er, yes, I also took out Vlad; as usual, nothing to report
there).  Turns out there'd been a gremlin playing in the wizard's moat,
and I didn't feel like bothering with that, so I pulled out my last ?
of genocide to get them.  The zoo on the second floor yielded up a + of
Cone of Cold, which I felt rounded out my collection of spells, and a
figurine of a vampire lord, which turned out to be blessed.  Since my
barbarian had had fun with his pet vampire lord Vinny, I soon found
myself playing with Vinny II.  Got to the wizard, zapped my wand of
death--and watched it miss.  Rodney then double-troubled and summoned
monsters, but they couldn't really get to me, so I zapped one of the
wizards and melee'd the second, grabbed the book and a plastic amulet,
whistled for Vinny II, and #invoked the orb down to the VS.  Performed
the ritual, went down the stairs, killed the wizard again, and then
tiptoed through the graveyard (one of the few times I've *really*
enjoyed being stealthy--I only had to kill a few zombies to make a
path, and I was able to leave the rest of the monsters asleep!).  On
the far end, I locked the door to the graveyard after going through it,
so I could whistle for Vinny without having a horde of newly-awakened
undead charging at me.  Tried to fight my way through the priests, but
quickly decided to use conflict instead; probably could've used /oTele,
but I forgot to consider them.  :-)

The door to the sanctum was, annoyingly enough, on the last wall I
checked, but I'd brought along a /oSDD specifically for that, so that
wasn't really an issue.  Got in, killed the priest, grabbed the amulet,
whistled for Vinny, and chugged my c!oGL.  The race was on!

Let me just say: I hate that mysterious force.  I got knocked back 13
times, and had to re-cross level 44 far more times than I thought
appropriate.  It was a lot better once I got past Orcus Town, though,
so I can't complain too much.  Did have to fight Rodney two more times,
but he never stole anything.  (One time, he disappeared upstairs as I
approached; guess he didn't realize that that's the direction I was
heading anyways...)

Stopped at the castle to drop the stuff I didn't want & grab the
scrolls and wands I'd prepared, and then ran on up.  Quaffed another
c!oGL at Medusa, since I didn't want to bother crossing it.  Rodney
showed up again once, and then I got to my final stash on level 1.  I
spent a fair amount of time here trying to sort out what I could take
with me and what I had to leave behind, but I eventually decided that
my K-rations and holy waters were going to leave me burdened anyhow, so
I finally just went for it.  Quaffed my third c!oGL out of paranoia,
and a desire to make a stylish exit.

Killed Rodney for the final time on Earth, and then used ?oGD to find
the portal.  I ended up using conflict for much of Air, though I'm not
sure how much it helped.  I'd only brought three ?oGD (silly me), so I
started out trying to find the portal on fire the hard way, but quickly
decided that wasn't working, so I used the Orb--good thing, too, since
the portal was close to where I started the level.  Unfortunately,
Vinny II got destroyed on fire.  *sigh*  Water was blindingly annoying,
even with ; genocided--I didn't have much to fear from the monsters,
but I couldn't find the bloomin' portal.  I went through my final ?oGD,
three charges of the Orb, and a couple shots of my wand of SDD before I
happened to stumble on the portal while checking a stack of items.
Sheesh.

I'd remembered to take off conflict, so I found myself with a new pet
on Astral; putting on my blindfold, I saw Death to the left, Famine in
the middle, and Pestilence on the right.  I was closest to the left,
though, so I decided to suck it up and deal with Death first.  Got over
to the door and tried to kick it down, only to be told that I didn't
have anything to brace against--I was still levitating!  Oops!  Quickly
swapped rings, kicked down the door, and found myself face-to-skull
with Death.  He died fairly quickly, only taking 5 off of my max HP,
which was nice; wands of teleport opened the way for me to get to the
temple, where I kicked down the door, walked up to the altar, and
checked--hey, it's Tyr's!  How about that?  Whistled for my Angel and
#offered!

